meta {
  name: Burn paste - second read is gone
  type: http
  seq: 5
  tags: [
    smoke
    burn
  ]
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/pastes/:id
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

params:path {
  id: {{burnPasteId}}
}

assert {
  res.status: 404
}

tests {
  test("says nothing about the paste having existed", function () {
    // [ApiController] turns NotFound() into ProblemDetails, so there is a body — it just
    // has nothing specific in it. Assert on what it does not contain, since that is the
    // property that matters.
    const body = res.getBody();

    expect(body.title).to.equal("Not Found");
    expect(body.status).to.equal(404);
    expect(body).to.not.have.property("detail");
    expect(JSON.stringify(body)).to.not.contain(bru.getVar("burnPasteId"));
  });
}

docs {
  Reading a burned paste again is a 404 — the same answer an id that never existed gets,
  and the same answer an expired one gets.

  That sameness is deliberate. Distinguishing "gone" from "never was" would confirm to
  someone walking the id space that a paste had once been there, which is information the
  server should not be handing out.

  The response does carry a body: `[ApiController]` renders `NotFound()` as ProblemDetails,
  so you get a generic `{"title":"Not Found","status":404}` plus a `traceId`. That is fine
  — it names no paste and describes no case. The test asserts the absence of a `detail`
  field and of the id rather than the absence of a body, because a future `NotFound("...")`
  with a helpful message is the change that would actually leak something.
}
